Uncertain Encounters, Many Possible Galaxies
==========================================

Open index.html directly (file://) or serve this directory with its sibling
TeachingTools directory. This is native, offline HTML/Canvas/JavaScript: no
Shiny, WebR, CDN, modules, installation or uploaded data. app.R and model.R
remain unchanged reference sources.

Principle: propagate encounter-input uncertainty into a family of forward
predictions. This is a RESTRICTED statistical encounter companion, NOT a
self-consistent N-body/galaxy-dynamics simulation or a replacement for the
separate HolmbergSimulation prototype.

Model
-----
Forty massless particles orbit a unit-mass softened host. A half-mass perturber
follows (-5 + speed*time, impact); the indirect term preserves the host-centred
frame. Softening=0.3. Kick-drift-kick leapfrog: 80 steps, dt=0.1. No live host,
disc self-gravity, gas, dynamical friction, deflection of the perturber or
adaptive integration. Input pairs are independent positive log-normals with
the selected arithmetic means and fractional SD. Twelve members share the
same initial particle disc and are compared at the same physical time.

The radius>2.5 fraction includes off-screen particles. It is a morphological
diagnostic, not escaped/unbound mass. The min-max band is the range of twelve
predictions, not a posterior or confidence interval. Explain contains guided
examples, limitations, the sampling equation and the original lecture link.

Native differences
------------------
The retained equations, particle/member counts, controls and 80-frame semantics
are preserved. A new small diagnostic plot shows the ensemble median/range
through elapsed time. All trajectories and summaries are computed once per
parameter set; playback only reveals them. New realisation advances a seeded
Astro RNG, Reset restores defaults. Browser and R RNG streams intentionally
differ; numeric integration is calibrated against retained-R deterministic
fixtures. Uncertainty slider step is 0.01 (rather than 0.02) so the original
0.35 close-encounter preset is valid, rather than silently snapping it.

Numerical API (model.js, browser ForwardModellingModel and CommonJS)
-----------------------------------------------------------------
defaults; STEPS=80; DT=0.1; MEMBERS=12
validate(parameters)
restrictedEncounter(radii, angles, impact, speed, steps=80, dt=0.1)
  -> trajectory[frame][particle][coordinate]
compute(parameters) -> nominal/ensemble trajectories, input draws, initial
  disc, per-frame summaries and integration metadata
atFrame(result, frame) -> positions, outside fractions, median/min/max,
  physical time and nominal perturber position; no randomness or integration
Angles use radians. Parameters are dimensionless. Invalid inputs throw
Astro.ValidationError. See defaults for all control names and accepted ranges.

Validation (from the repository root)
------------------------------------
node ForwardModelling/test_model.js
node ForwardModelling/test_browser.js
Numerical tests use retained-R trajectory and R qlnorm input-draw fixtures, an independent nearly
circular orbit / dt-convergence limit, exact zero-uncertainty collapse,
seed stability, cached frames and control boundaries. The shared Chrome
runner additionally covers real controls, plots, invalid input, preset 0.35,
tabs, reset, playback, mobile and direct file:// access.

The browser runner uses os.tmpdir() for its Chrome profile. If temporary system
paths are unavailable, create ForwardModelling/.browser-state, set TMPDIR to its
absolute path for the test command, then remove that empty directory.

Runtime assets: index.html, model.js, app.js, plus
../TeachingTools/{statistics.js,browser.js,browser.css,lesson.js}. No others.
